{% doc %}
  Defines window.PTTrack, the shared search-analytics helper every tire/wheel
  search surface uses to push a consistent event set onto GTM's dataLayer.

  GTM is the only analytics transport on this store (see snippets/pt-analytics)
  — there is no gtag / PostHog call anywhere — so this pushes plain objects onto
  window.dataLayer and does nothing else. No network request of its own.

  Outputs a plain inline script tag (not a javascript tag block) so the global
  exists before the surface scripts run. Safe to render more than once: only the
  first copy defines the global.

  API:
    PTTrack.push(event, params)       → dataLayer.push({event, ...params}), never throws
    PTTrack.once(key, event, params)  → same, but drops a repeat of the same key
                                        within 1.5s. This is a re-entrancy guard
                                        for a double-bound handler or a re-render,
                                        NOT the de-duplication mechanism — see
                                        "WHAT COUNTS AS A SEARCH" below.
    PTTrack.go(url)                   → navigate, but give the (lazy-loaded) GTM
                                        container a bounded chance to drain the
                                        queue first, so an event pushed on the
                                        way out is not thrown away with the page.

  Params are normalised on the way out so every surface reports the same shapes:
  make/model/trim lowercased + trimmed, size upper-cased without spaces, year and
  the *_count / *_len / status fields numeric, and empty values dropped.

  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  WHAT COUNTS AS A SEARCH

  Exactly one pt_search_submit + one pt_search_result per search, emitted when
  the search FINISHES. A search finishes when either:

    • the customer commits — presses Enter or the search arrow, clicks a
      rendered result row, or clicks a "Shop tires" / submit button; or
    • it dead-ends — we found nothing, or we know the vehicle but cannot size
      it, so there is nothing for the customer to commit to.

  Steps on the way to a commit emit NOTHING: keystrokes, the 300 ms live-suggest
  render, and walking a year → make → model → trim cascade are not searches.
  This is what makes surface volumes comparable — otherwise a dropdown surface
  would score one "search" per click while a free-text surface scored one per
  committed query.

  Two consequences worth knowing when reading the data:
    • A dead end reached while the customer is still typing is held briefly and
      cancelled if they keep typing, so an abandoned prefix ("2024 hond") or a
      corrected typo is not counted as its own failed search.
    • Re-playing a saved search from the URL (the ?ts_* deep link that
      pt-tire-search rebuilds on load, and every reload / back-navigation of
      such a page) emits nothing at all — the customer did not search.
      The hero's ?ss= hand-off DOES emit, with method 'deep_link', because a
      customer really did search on the previous page.
  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  EVENT SCHEMA (snake_case, stable; vehicle + size only, never a customer
  identifier, and never the raw free-text query):

    pt_search_submit  { surface, method, year?, make?, model?, trim?, size?, query_len? }
      A customer committed a search, or ran into a dead end.

    pt_search_result  { surface, method, outcome, sizes_count, trims_count?,
                        status?, year?, make?, model?, size? }
      What that search produced. Always paired 1:1 with a pt_search_submit.
      outcome: 'sizes'      → we resolved usable tire size(s); sizes_count > 0
               'browse'     → no size, but the query did resolve to a filtered
                              collection (a brand and/or a season). Neither a
                              sized success nor a dead end. A parser outage
                              shows up here as a spike, because a query with a
                              brand in it falls back to a brand filter.
               'no_sizes'   → we know the vehicle but cannot size it (no OEM data
                              for the trim, trim still missing, size unparseable)
               'not_found'  → we could not resolve the input to a vehicle or a
                              size (parser miss, vehicle absent from the data,
                              VIN unrecognised)
               'data_error' → OUR fault, not the customer's: the per-year vehicle
                              file could not be loaded (404 / network / bad JSON).
                              assets/pt-vehicle-*.json is .shopifyignore'd and is
                              published only to the MAIN theme by the fitment
                              pipeline, so every year pick on a dev or freshly
                              published theme lands here. Keep this OUT of any
                              customer-failure rate.

      sizes_count  distinct OEM tire sizes the search resolved (1 for a tire-size
                   search, 0 on every outcome other than 'sizes').
      trims_count  how many trims were offered, when we showed a trim list. Six
                   trims that share one OEM size are trims_count 6, sizes_count 1.
      status       HTTP status, on 'data_error' where we have one (0 = no response).

      NOTE: outcome 'sizes' means "we resolved a size the customer can shop",
      NOT "the collection behind that size has stock". A perfectly parseable
      size with nothing in inventory is still 'sizes' here. Measure the
      empty-collection rate on the collection page, not from this event.

    pt_missing_vehicle { surface, trigger, year?, make?, model? }
      The "tell us your vehicle" capture ran.
      trigger: 'auto'     → the surface POSTed the lead on the customer's behalf
                            after a failed search; they did nothing. Fires at
                            roughly the rate of unrecognised queries.
               'customer' → the customer filled the form and pressed send.
      Never sum the two triggers into one number: they are different acts.

    surface: hero | tire_search | collection_strip | collection_grid | garage | chat
    method:  text | dropdown | vin | saved_vehicle | deep_link

  Reading the events: a search is "by vehicle" when year/make/model are present,
  "by tire size" when only size is. query_len stands in for the free-text query
  (privacy + noise); the query itself is never sent.

  PRIVACY / CONSENT
  The payloads carry no direct identifier and never the raw query, the raw VIN,
  or the free-text vehicle the customer typed into the capture form (those
  appear only as in-closure de-dupe keys, never in a pushed object). But
  surface 'garage' is served ONLY to a signed-in customer (sections/my-garage
  renders inside a customer guard), so a year/make/model tuple from there is a
  quasi-identifier attached to a session the store has already identified. For
  that reason the garage deliberately omits `trim` and `size` from its payloads
  — surface + outcome + sizes_count is enough to answer "does the garage dead-
  end people". PTTrack performs no consent check of its own: it assumes GTM
  Consent Mode is configured correctly in the container, which lives outside
  this theme. If a consent tool is ever added, gate these pushes on Shopify's
  Customer Privacy API before trusting the container's defaults.

  Note: this layer is additive. The older, surface-specific `pt_search` (via
  PTTireParse.track) and `tire_search_*` (via pt.track) events still fire and are
  unchanged; only these three events are comparable across all surfaces.

  @example
  {% render 'pt-search-tracking' %}
{% enddoc %}
<script>
  (function () {
    if (window.PTTrack) return;

    var LOWER = ['make', 'model', 'trim'];
    var NUM = ['year', 'sizes_count', 'trims_count', 'query_len', 'status'];
    var seen = {};

    function clean(params) {
      var out = {};
      if (!params) return out;
      for (var k in params) {
        if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(params, k)) continue;
        var v = params[k];
        if (v === null || v === undefined || v === '') continue;
        if (LOWER.indexOf(k) !== -1) v = String(v).trim().toLowerCase();
        else if (k === 'size') v = String(v).trim().toUpperCase().replace(/\s+/g, '');
        else if (NUM.indexOf(k) !== -1) { v = parseInt(v, 10); if (isNaN(v)) continue; }
        if (v === '') continue;
        out[k] = v;
      }
      return out;
    }

    function push(event, params) {
      try {
        if (!event) return;
        var payload = clean(params);
        payload.event = event;
        window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
        window.dataLayer.push(payload);
      } catch (e) { /* analytics must never break a search */ }
    }

    // Re-entrancy guard only: drops a repeat of the same logical action (a
    // re-render, a double-bound handler) fired within 1.5s of the first. The
    // surfaces are responsible for emitting once per search in the first place.
    function once(key, event, params) {
      try {
        var id = event + '|' + key;
        var now = +new Date();
        if (seen[id] && now - seen[id] < 1500) return;
        seen[id] = now;
      } catch (e) { /* fall through and push */ }
      push(event, params);
    }

    /* ── Navigating away without losing the event ──────────────────────────
       gtm.js is ~1.7MB, so snippets/pt-analytics deliberately defers it: the
       container only starts downloading on the first pointerdown / keydown /
       touchstart / scroll (or a 3.5s idle fallback), and until it has run,
       window.dataLayer is nothing but an array in this page's memory. A
       surface that pushes and then sets window.location in the same tick
       throws that array away with the document — and the loss is biased,
       because the searches that succeed are exactly the ones that navigate,
       while the ones that dead-end sit on the page long enough to be recorded.

       go() hands the container a bounded chance to take over first. It costs
       nothing in the common case (the customer already typed or tapped, so the
       container is running) and is skipped entirely when no container is
       configured, so a customer is never held on the page waiting for GTM. */
    var GO_MAX_WAIT = 400; // ms — hard cap on the wait before we navigate anyway
    var GO_POLL = 25;      // ms
    var kicked = false;

    // gtm.js defines this once it has executed and taken over dataLayer.
    function containerRunning() {
      try { return !!window.google_tag_manager; } catch (e) { return false; }
    }
    // pt-analytics pushes {'gtm.start': …, event: 'gtm.js'} at the top of the
    // page when — and only when — a container ID is configured.
    function containerExpected() {
      try {
        var dl = window.dataLayer;
        if (!dl || typeof dl.length !== 'number') return false;
        for (var i = 0; i < dl.length; i++) if (dl[i] && dl[i].event === 'gtm.js') return true;
      } catch (e) { /* hostile dataLayer — assume no container */ }
      return false;
    }
    function kickContainer() {
      if (kicked) return;
      kicked = true;
      try {
        // pt-analytics starts gtm.js on the first of its four listeners. A
        // non-bubbling 'scroll' dispatched at the document reaches that one
        // and nothing on window.
        document.dispatchEvent(new Event('scroll'));
      } catch (e) { /* synthetic events unsupported — the cap covers us */ }
    }

    function go(url) {
      var navigated = false;
      function nav() {
        if (navigated) return;
        navigated = true;
        try { window.location.href = url; } catch (e) { /* nothing left to do */ }
      }
      if (!url) return;
      try {
        if (containerRunning() || !containerExpected()) { nav(); return; }
        kickContainer();
        var waited = 0;
        var timer = setInterval(function () {
          waited += GO_POLL;
          if (containerRunning() || waited >= GO_MAX_WAIT) { clearInterval(timer); nav(); }
        }, GO_POLL);
      } catch (e) { nav(); }
    }

    window.PTTrack = { push: push, once: once, go: go };
  })();
</script>
